exports[`canonical queue skill (AWTD-759) > pins the old skill and queue line while the master switch is off, even for an enrolled board (AWTD-1557) > claim-disabled legacy queue line 1`] = `"Call get_agent_queue with agent "codex" and listId "<board>". Work every task it returns to completion, commenting progress on each one. If it answers empty:true, stop and say nothing is queued."`;

exports[`canonical queue skill (AWTD-759) > pins the old skill and queue line while the master switch is off, even for an enrolled board (AWTD-1557) > claim-disabled legacy skill 1`] = `
"Astrid queue skill v1.2.0

Call get_agent_queue with agent "codex" and listId "<board>". Work every task it returns to completion, commenting progress on each one. If it answers empty:true, stop and say nothing is queued.

Setup: connect this harness to Astrid MCP and install this skill using the guided steps at /docs/loops. This skill describes queue behavior; it does not install or start an agent host.

Mailbox and board are explicit choices, never defaults:
- Mailbox: this loop is agent "codex" — never poll another identity's queue.
- Board: always pass listId "<board>" so this loop never takes another board's work.

Per task, in queue order:
1. Read the task AND its comments (get_task, get_task_comments) before changing anything.
2. Post a short strategy comment (add_comment), then work the task to completion.
3. Finish with a completion report in plain language, then update_task completed:true.
4. Re-check the queue with the same call before the next task — new or reopened tasks arrive mid-run.

Boundaries:
- Only tasks assigned to "codex" in Ready status are yours. get_agent_queue already filters for this; never work around it. An unassigned task is someone's untriaged note.
- If you do not use the board columns at all, do not work around that either — pass requireReady: false and a task with NO status queues on assignment alone. Waiting and Doing are still never queued under it, so parking a blocked task in Waiting still stops the loop re-reading it.
- A task with a future date is held automatically (held.scheduled) — report it, do not start it early.
- empty:true means the run is DONE: stop and say nothing is queued. It also carries a "hint" naming the condition that is unmet — usually tasks assigned to "codex" that nobody set to Ready. Report the hint rather than a bare "nothing queued"; it is the difference between a quiet day and a setup that has never worked.
- If a tool call fails or a task is blocked, say so visibly on the task and stop or move on — never retry silently in a loop, and never invent adjacent work to fill a quiet run."
`;
